WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.572
CSIS Oversight Failures.

00:00:02.522 --> 00:00:05.090
Intelligence without accountability.

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:09.034
CSIS documented foreign interference in Canadian elections.

00:00:09.034 --> 00:00:13.886
The intelligence reached senior government officials including the Prime Minister's Office.

00:00:13.886 --> 00:00:16.295
The response was insufficient.

00:00:16.295 --> 00:00:21.613
The Hogue Commission confirmed the intelligence flow worked — the problem was that no one acted on it.

00:00:21.613 --> 00:00:25.738
NSICOP identified MPs wittingly assisting foreign states.

00:00:25.738 --> 00:00:28.136
The names remain classified.

00:00:28.136 --> 00:00:33.943
The accountability gap between intelligence collection and democratic protection is where the system fails.

00:00:33.943 --> 00:00:37.090
Canada's intelligence oversight architecture.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:41.511
CSIS collects intelligence and reports to the Minister of Public Safety.

00:00:41.511 --> 00:00:44.318
NSIRA provides independent review.

00:00:44.318 --> 00:00:48.420
NSICOP provides parliamentary oversight with security clearances.

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:52.374
The CEIPP panel monitors election-period threats.

00:00:52.374 --> 00:00:55.193
Each body can investigate and report.

00:00:55.193 --> 00:00:57.193
None can prosecute.

00:00:57.193 --> 00:01:00.931
The architecture is designed for review, not enforcement.

00:01:00.931 --> 00:01:02.738
The failure point.

00:01:02.738 --> 00:01:09.534
The Hogue Commission found that intelligence about foreign interference reached senior government officials but the response was insufficient.

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:14.090
CSIS did its job — it collected the intelligence and communicated it upward.

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:18.318
The failure occurred between intelligence receipt and government action.

00:01:18.318 --> 00:01:23.193
The system has no mechanism to force the government to act on intelligence it receives.

00:01:23.193 --> 00:01:25.852
The accountability architecture gap.

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:33.204
Canada has five oversight bodies that can investigate intelligence failures, but only one, the RCMP, can prosecute.

00:01:33.204 --> 00:01:37.579
The RCMP has laid zero charges for foreign interference.

00:01:37.579 --> 00:01:41.090
The system is designed for review, not enforcement.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:46.693
Intelligence is collected, reports are written, recommendations are made, and nothing changes.

00:01:46.693 --> 00:01:54.374
This is the same pattern documented across every TENET5 investigation: document the failure, promise reform, never prosecute.
